WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
An Ollama installation should be treated as a managed service,

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.900
not just a one-time tool download.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.390
Teams should know how it was installed, how the service starts, how it stops,

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:11.500
which user account it runs under, and where its runtime state lives.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.295
Those details matter when troubleshooting a failed startup, a missing model,

00:00:15.295 --> 00:00:18.700
a permission problem, or a configuration mismatch between machines.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.700
Service lifecycle practices are especially important when a local tool becomes part of

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.455
shared work.

00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:25.445
A developer workstation, a lab server,

00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:28.455
and a production-like host may all need different settings.

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:30.500
A workstation may favor flexibility.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.485
A shared server may need predictable startup, controlled network exposure,

00:00:33.485 --> 00:00:35.500
documented storage, and support expectations.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.735
A production-like host may require tighter change control and monitoring.

00:00:39.735 --> 00:00:42.950
Environment variables and service configuration should be documented because small

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.455
differences can change behavior.

00:00:44.455 --> 00:00:48.305
Storage locations, bind addresses, model cache behavior, runtime directories,

00:00:48.305 --> 00:00:50.475
and service users can all affect operations.

00:00:50.475 --> 00:00:52.530
If one machine behaves differently from another,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.455
the answer is often in configuration rather than in the model itself.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:59.360
Logs and service status checks are the first place to look when the runtime does not

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.455
behave as expected.

00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:04.480
Clear lifecycle practices make upgrades, restarts, troubleshooting, and support easier.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.860
They also help new team members understand the environment without depending on tribal

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.480
knowledge.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.416
It is also helpful to separate development assumptions from shared-service assumptions.

00:01:12.416 --> 00:01:16.640
A developer may restart a local service freely, change settings for a test,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.475
or remove models to save space.

00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:22.710
A shared host needs a more predictable process so active users, dependent applications,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.500
and support staff understand what is changing and when.
